RUNWAY NOTES - MONDAY SHORT
Monday, March 16, 2026

The mailbag got long. Four questions, short answers. The regular letter is Thursday.

"Should we report ARR or annualized revenue if 30% of our revenue is usage?" Report both, define both in a footnote, and never let anyone say ARR without the definition attached. The companies that get in trouble are the ones whose ARR definition changed between rounds.

"A customer wants a 20% discount for a three-year prepaid contract. Take it?" Price the discount against your cost of capital, not against list. If a bank would lend you the same money at 12%, a 20% discount for cash up front is expensive money. Offer 10% for prepaid, or 20% for a three-year commitment billed annually, and let them choose.

"We have a services line that is 15% of revenue at 30% gross margin. Hide it in COGS or break it out?" Break it out. Investors can do arithmetic; what they cannot do is trust a blended margin they know is blended. A visible services line at 30% is fine. A software margin that turns out to include services is a diligence problem.

"How many quota-carrying reps should a $4M ARR company have?" Fewer than you think. Median in our set at that stage is three, with the founder still closing the largest deals. Companies that went to six or more reps before $6M had a worse burn multiple two years later in almost every case.

Back Thursday.

